# Agent harness

The agent harness runs maintainability checks on **new and changed code only**, and gives
coding agents (and humans) fast, specific feedback. It never asks anyone to refactor code they
did not touch. `AGENTS.md` tells agents how to work with it; this document explains how it
works, how to tune it, and how to add it to an existing repository.

## How it works

### One entry point

Every adapter ({% if enable_claude_adapter %}Claude Code hooks, {% endif %}lefthook, CI) calls the same script:

| Command | Used by | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `scripts/check.sh` | Stop hook, lefthook, CI, agents | Full gate on the changed set |
| `scripts/check.sh --file <path>` | Post-edit hook | Fast checks (Ruff, complexipy, suppressions) for one file |
| `scripts/check.sh --mutation` | CI job, on demand | Mutation testing on changed lines (slow) |
| `scripts/check.sh --all` | Humans | Whole-codebase audit; report only, always exits 0 |
| `scripts/check.sh --json` | Tooling | Every finding as JSON (the text report stops at 40) |
| `scripts/check.sh --timings` | Humans | Per-tool timings on stderr |

Exit codes: **0** passed, **1** a blocking finding, **3** the harness or a tool could not run.
A missing tool, or a tool whose output cannot be parsed, is exit 3, never a silent pass.

The implementation is `scripts/harness/`, standard library only. Each tool has one module in
`scripts/harness/runners/`; independent runners run in parallel.

### What "changed" means

The base is the first of these that works:

1. The `HARNESS_BASE` environment variable (CI sets it to the merge-base of the PR).
2. `git merge-base HEAD origin/{{ default_branch }}`.
3. `git merge-base HEAD {{ default_branch }}`.
4. `HEAD`, so only uncommitted changes count.
5. With no commits yet, the empty tree: everything is new.

The changed set is the diff from the base to the **working tree** (committed, staged, unstaged
and untracked `.py` files under `src/` and `tests/`).

A finding is **in scope** when it is on an added or modified line, or anywhere inside a
function or class whose span contains such a line (class-level findings such as coupling use
the enclosing class). New files are entirely in scope. So touching a function makes you
responsible for that function's findings, including old ones, and nothing else.

Two checks cannot be scoped and always run on the whole project: import-linter contracts and
import cycles.

### Checks

| Check | Tool | Threshold | Gate | Configured in |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cognitive complexity | complexipy | ≤ 15 per function | Block | `complexipy.toml` |
| Cyclomatic complexity | Ruff `C901` | ≤ 10 | Block | `[tool.ruff.lint.mccabe]` |
| Size and shape | Ruff `PLR09xx`, `PLR1702` | 5 args, 5 positional, 6 returns, 12 branches, 50 statements | Block | `[tool.ruff.lint.pylint]` |
| Exception hygiene | Ruff `E722 BLE001 S110 S112 B904 B012 TRY002 TRY203 TRY400 TRY401` | 0 | Block | `harness.toml [ruff]` |
| Docstring presence | Ruff `D100–D104 D106` | public names | Block | `harness.toml [ruff]` |
| Docstring consistency | pydoclint ({{ docstring_style }} style) | 0 | Block | `[tool.pydoclint]` |
| Type precision | mypy, strict | 0 errors | Block | `[tool.mypy]`, `harness.toml [mypy]` |
| Suppressions | harness | code and `# reason:` required | Block | — |
| Dead code | vulture | see below | Block / advisory | `harness.toml [vulture]`, `vulture_whitelist.py` |
| Architecture contracts | import-linter | all kept | Block (whole project) | `.importlinter` |
| Import cycles | pyscn | ≤ `allowed_cycles` (0) | Block (whole project) | `harness.toml [pyscn]` |
| Class coupling (CBO) | pyscn | ≤ 7 | Block | `harness.toml [pyscn]` |
| Class cohesion (LCOM4) | pyscn | > 5 blocks, > 2 advises | Block / advisory | `harness.toml [pyscn]` |
| Structural clones | pyscn | similarity ≥ 0.80 | Advisory | `harness.toml [pyscn]` |
| Changelog reminder | harness | `src/` changed, no `[Unreleased]` entry | Advisory | — |
| Mutation survival | cosmic-ray | ≤ 20 % of executed mutants | CI job, advisory for now | `harness.toml [mutation]`, `cosmic-ray.toml` |
| Churn × complexity | git + complexipy | top 10 | Weekly report | `scripts/hotspots.sh` |

Details worth knowing:

- **Ruff.** The harness runs Ruff with `--extend-select` and the rule list in `harness.toml`,
  so it works even where `pyproject.toml` does not select those rules. `PLR0904`, `PLR0914`,
  `PLR0916`, `PLR0917` and `PLR1702` are preview rules in the pinned Ruff; the harness enables
  preview for its own run only, and only for those named rules. `ruff check .` in `build.sh`
  never runs in preview mode.
- **Dead code.** A library's public API looks unused from inside the package, so vulture runs
  over `src`, `tests` and `vulture_whitelist.py` (tested API counts as used). Findings at
  ≥ 80 % confidence block, as do unused private (`_name`) definitions; unused public names are
  advisory. Unused local variables are rated 60 % by vulture, so in public code they are
  advisory here, but Ruff `F841` in `build.sh` already blocks them.
- **Suppression policy.** On added lines, `# type: ignore` and `# noqa` need a specific code,
  and every `# type: ignore[...]`, `# noqa: ...`, `# pragma: no cover`, `# pragma: no mutate`
  or `# complexipy: ignore` needs `# reason: <text>` on the same line or the line above. The
  report counts suppressions added in the diff even when all are justified. pyscn has no
  inline-ignore syntax, so there is nothing to police for it.
- **Coupling and cohesion** skip dataclasses, `Protocol`s, enums, `NamedTuple`/`TypedDict`
  and exception classes, which produce false positives.
- **Clones** are reported once per duplicated region: overlapping pairs from the same
  functions are merged.

### Advisories

Advisories never fail lefthook or CI.{% if enable_claude_adapter %} The Claude Code Stop hook
blocks **once** when advisories are present, so the agent either fixes each one or justifies it
in its final message; on the forced retry advisories are ignored.{% endif %} Agents without
hooks see them when they run `scripts/check.sh`, as `AGENTS.md` requires.

### Configuration agents must not change

`harness.toml`, `complexipy.toml`, `.pyscn.toml`, `.importlinter`, `cosmic-ray.toml`,
`vulture_whitelist.py`, `lefthook.yml`, `scripts/check.sh`, `scripts/harness/` and `.claude/`.
The list lives in `scripts/harness/guarded.py`.{% if enable_claude_adapter %} The Claude Code
PreToolUse hook asks you before an agent edits any of them.{% endif %} CI adds a notice to
the PR summary when a PR changes one of them, or the `[tool.ruff]`, `[tool.mypy]` or
`[tool.pydoclint]` settings in `pyproject.toml` (which is not guarded as a file, because
agents legitimately edit dependencies).

## Adapters
{%- if enable_claude_adapter %}

### Claude Code

`.claude/settings.json` registers four hooks (stdlib Python, run with
`uv run --no-sync python`):

| Hook | Script | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| PostToolUse `Edit\|Write` | `post_edit.py` | `check.sh --file` on an edited `.py` file under `src/` or `tests/`. Findings: exit 2, shown to Claude. Harness error: exit 1, shown to you; the agent carries on |
| Stop | `stop.py` | Skips when the changed set is unchanged since the last pass (`.harness/last-pass`). Otherwise runs the gate; on failures or advisories blocks once with the report and an instruction to fix only touched code. On the retry it never blocks: if the gate still fails you get a `systemMessage` |
| PreToolUse `Edit\|Write` | `guard_config.py` | Asks you before an agent edits harness configuration |
| PreToolUse `Bash` | `guard_git.py` | Asks you before `git add/commit/push/tag/merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert/am` or `reset/restore/clean/stash` and path checkouts. Read-only commands pass. Commands are parsed (`git -C`, `&&`, `bash -c`, `env`, aliases); when parsing is uncertain it asks |

`CLAUDE.md` contains `@AGENTS.md`. Recent Claude Code versions read `AGENTS.md` themselves when
there is no `CLAUDE.md`; keeping the import never makes it read the file twice.
{%- endif %}

### Adding another agent

Wire the agent's post-edit event to `scripts/check.sh --file <path>` and its end-of-task event
to `scripts/check.sh`, translating exit code 1 into whatever that agent treats as blocking
feedback, and exit code 3 into a message for the human (never a block). Agents with no hook
system rely on `AGENTS.md` and the lefthook hooks.

### lefthook

- `pre-commit` runs `scripts/build.sh`, which ends with `scripts/check.sh`.
- `pre-push` runs `scripts/check.sh` again against the merge-base, a second net for commits
  made with `--no-verify`.

### GitHub Actions

- `pr.yml` checks out full history, sets `HARNESS_BASE` to the merge-base with the target
  branch, adds the configuration notice and a non-blocking cognitive-complexity diff
  (`complexipy --diff-only`) to the job summary, and runs `build.sh`.
- The `mutation` job runs `scripts/check.sh --mutation` with a 20-minute timeout. It is
  `continue-on-error` for now. **Make it required** (remove `continue-on-error` and add it to
  branch protection) once the survival rate on PRs has been stable for a few weeks.
- `push.yml` runs the build on the default branch, where the changed set is empty.
- `hotspots.yml` runs weekly and on demand, and uploads `reports/hotspots.md`.

## Mutation testing

`scripts/check.sh --mutation` runs cosmic-ray on lines changed since the base, including new,
untracked files. cosmic-ray's git filter only sees tracked files and a fixed branch, so the
harness points it at the resolved base and lists new files in a temporary git index
(`.harness/mutation.index`, intent-to-add entries; your real index is never touched). The
survival rate counts executed mutants only. Each surviving mutant is reported with its line and
the change that went unnoticed, so the missing assertion is easy to write.

cosmic-ray **mutates source files in place** while it runs tests and restores them afterwards.
If you interrupt a run, check `git diff` and restore any mutated file.

## Hotspots

`scripts/hotspots.sh [--days 90] [--top 10]` ranks files under `src/` by commits in the window
× total cognitive complexity and writes `reports/hotspots.md`, with the three most complex
functions per file. Use it to prioritise deliberate refactoring; it never fails. For trends,
keep each weekly report and compare them.

## Known gaps

- Ruff does not flag a narrow handler that returns a default (`except KeyError: return None`);
  that is often correct and is left to review.
- Neither tool can tell whether a docstring is useful; `AGENTS.md` sets the expectation.
- Deleting lines does not, by itself, put the surrounding function in scope.
- mypy checks the package; type errors in `tests/` are not gated (as in `build.sh`).
{%- if enable_claude_adapter %}
- `guard_config.py` sees Edit and Write only; an agent could still change a guarded file through
  a shell command. The CI notice is the backstop.
- Claude Code treats a crashing PreToolUse hook as non-blocking, so if `uv` or Python is
  missing the guards stop guarding.
{%- endif %}
- Every new public function without a test yields an "unused" advisory until it is tested.

## Future work: semantic duplication

pyscn finds structural clones. Code that does the same job with a different shape is caught
only by the `AGENTS.md` rule to search before writing. An LLM review step or embedding search
over function bodies could close that gap; it is deliberately out of scope for now.

## Retrofitting an existing repository

Easiest: generate a scratch project with this template, using your package name and default
branch, and copy the harness files from it, so the names are already right.

1. **Copy files:** `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `harness.toml`, `complexipy.toml`,
   `.pyscn.toml`, `.importlinter`, `cosmic-ray.toml`, `vulture_whitelist.py`, `.claude/`,
   `scripts/check.sh`, `scripts/hotspots.sh`, `scripts/harness/`, `docs/agent-harness.md`,
   `docs/troubleshooting.md`, `docs/adr/0000-template.md` and
   `.github/workflows/hotspots.yml`. Add a `CHANGELOG.md` in Keep a Changelog format if the
   repository has none. Keep the executable bit on the scripts.
2. **Edit names** if you did not copy from a scratch project: `package` and `default_branch` in
   `harness.toml`, `root_packages` in `.importlinter`, `module-path` and `branch` in
   `cosmic-ray.toml`.
3. **Add dev dependencies:** `complexipy` (with `python = "<3.16"`, which complexipy declares),
   `pyscn`, `import-linter`, `vulture`, `cosmic-ray`, `pydoclint`.
4. **Merge config into `pyproject.toml`:** `[tool.ruff.lint.mccabe]`,
   `[tool.ruff.lint.pylint]`, `[tool.ruff.lint.pydocstyle]` (omit for sphinx style, which Ruff
   does not support), the `"tests/**"` entry of `[tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]` (so tests
   need no docstrings) and `[tool.pydoclint]`. Do **not** add the harness rules to `select`;
   the harness adds them itself, so the existing whole-project lint is unaffected. Do not turn
   on strict mypy globally unless the project is already clean: put the strict flags in
   `harness.toml` under `[mypy] flags` (an example is in the file), so they apply to changed
   code only.
5. **Grandfather whole-project checks:** run `lint-imports` and add current violations to
   each contract's `ignore_imports`; run `scripts/check.sh --all`, read the cycle count from the
   `import-cycle` findings, and set `allowed_cycles` in `harness.toml` to it. Both then act as
   ratchets: lower them as you fix things.
6. **Seed the vulture whitelist:** `vulture src tests --make-whitelist`, then review every entry.
7. **Wire the adapters:** add `./scripts/check.sh` to the end of your build script (or as a
   lefthook `pre-commit` command), add the `pre-push` command, and apply the CI changes above
   (`fetch-depth: 0`, `HARNESS_BASE`, the notice, complexity summary and mutation job).
8. **Run `scripts/check.sh --all`** once to see the size of the existing debt. It is a report
   only; do not start a clean-up because of it.
9. **Append `.gitignore` entries:** `.harness/`, `.complexipy_cache/`,
   `.import_linter_cache/`, `.pyscn/`, `reports/`, `.claude/settings.local.json`. Keep
   `.claude/settings.json` committed.

Projects generated from this template before it produced `.copier-answers.yml` cannot use
`copier update`; retrofit them with this checklist instead.
